# `index.html` — the main file

One self-contained page: ~29,000 lines, ~2.3 MB, ~60 views, the "Instrument" system. No
build step, no external runtime dependencies. Open it in a browser to run it.

## How to work in it

- **Never read the whole file.** Read the region you need. Locate first with `grep -n`,
  then read a bounded range.
- **Never pass replacement code through the shell.** Quotes get stripped silently and a
  syntax check still passes, so the damage surfaces later and far from its cause. Write
  the new text to a file, splice it in with a Node script, and **assert on unique anchor
  strings** — the script should refuse to write if an anchor matches zero times or more
  than once.
- **Scope every new CSS rule under `#fxRoot`** (or the owning view's id). `.dw-hd`,
  `.dw-tabs`, `.dw-body` and `.dw-foot` are shared class names between the Flux review
  workspace and the GL ledger inspector (`.acctsurf .gl-detail`); an unscoped selector
  restyles the other one.
- **Verify in the browser, not by reading.** Drive the view, assert the DOM, check the
  console. Geometry in this file is *measured* at runtime (`sizeDrawer`, `syncStick`), so
  reasoning about the CSS alone will mislead you.

## Structural facts worth knowing before you edit

- `<script id="egl-data">` is a **baked data snapshot**, not an import. It is serialised
  by `packages/core/tools/emit_enterprise_gl.mjs` and read by exactly one view,
  `view-egl`. This is the only bridge between `packages/` and the prototypes, and it is
  build-time and one-directional.
- The Flux review workspace renders at **two densities from one set of renderers**:
  `dwCtx()` derives every figure once per paint, `RWSEC` is a registry of named parts, and
  `RW_LAYOUT` is a pure composition table for everything except Overview. Overview is
  state-composed by `dwModeOf` / `dwOverviewKeys` (inspect / explain / review / locked).
  `RW_LAYOUT.drawer.sum` is the material-line default those keys return — not the only
  Overview. A part absent from `RWSEC` cannot render in either density. Do not fork it
  into two builders. Any refactor there must pass the byte-identity probe: hash
  `#fxDwBody.innerHTML` for every tab on every line before and after; they must be
  identical strings.
- **Four Overview modes, and a freeze.** inspect (`!r.req`) reads — drivers, a quiet
  optional-explanation cue, no footer. explain (`r.req`, draft/returned) works — the
  explanation owns Save/Accept; the footer is Submit once coverage clears. review
  (`submitted`) and locked (`approved`) report. Submitted reads exactly like locked in
  the explanation card (`canPrep = !lock && !frozen && caps().prepare`): a preparer
  must not rewrite the words a reviewer is reading. The way back is the reviewer's
  Return, a recorded transition — name the freeze rather than silently dropping Edit.
  Every mode's Overview ends with a common tail — Workflow & review, Evidence, Comments
  (`dwOverviewKeys` appends `['reviewer','evidRow','cmtPeek']`) — each a collapsed
  summary with a chevron to its own tab, mirroring the `apps/review` inspector. The
  header leads with a hero variance (a big signed figure + Δ% over a prior → current
  balance flow); key drivers render as a contribution bar chart. Colour on variance
  only. Both densities read as a **light dashboard** (owner's
  direction): the drawer is white hairline blocks on a `--bg2` body; the full page
  is a white header over a gray body with the three columns as elevated white cards.
  Depth is lightness + a soft shadow (relaxing the old "one card, no shadow inside"
  ruling for this panel). Drawer card CSS is scoped `:not(.dw-page)`, page card CSS
  is scoped `.dw-page`, so neither leaks into the other. A derived classification
  pill (Routine / Material · explained / Needs review) and a reviewer initials
  avatar sit on the Explanation and Workflow blocks respectively.
- **Flux filters are inline and TABBED BY CATEGORY, not a drawer** (2026-08-28, owner's
  direction). The shared `#filterbar` drawer is hidden on Flux (`paintTopbar()` gates on
  `fxHere`); filters live in `fxFilterFields()` → `.fx-filters` inside `#fxRoot`, as a tab
  strip over ONE contextual row: `.fx-filters` = `.fx-ftabs` + `.fx-fields`, nothing else.
  `FX_FTABS` = Period · Scope · Workflow · Variance · Basis · Display · Saved Views · Actions;
  the last three are `cfg:1` (configure, not filter — never counted). Filter fields are a fixed
  **180px × 28px × 6px radius on every tab** — pin the 6px explicitly, never inherit
  `var(--radius)`, because `body.fx-mercury #fxRoot.aw` raises it to 14px and turns a 28px
  control into a capsule (same trap for `.fx-fields .seg`); long values ellipsise and the
  tooltip carries `label · value`. **There is no second
  toolbar and there must not be one**: `pageActions()`/`viewControls()` are retired, Display
  owns mode + Table. **Two display modes only** — `stmt` (Statement) and `full` (Narrative);
  Review and Financials merged once they differed by a single column. Every retired key
  (`status`, `nums`, `compact`, `review`, `cmt`) migrates on READ in `dispMode()` and anything
  unrecognised falls back to Statement, never Narrative. The keyboard legend and handler both
  derive from `DISP_MODES.length` — do not hard-code `1–3`. Display Saved Views owns the star + Views menu, Actions owns the ellipsis.
  Rejected placements: above the tabs, below the fields, and the shell topbar. **Workflow**
  not "Review" (Review is a display mode); **Variance** not "Movement". Submit is the one CTA
  and renders ONLY when lines are ready, stating the count ("Submit 3 ready lines") — the
  count and `submitAll()` share `readyToSubmit()` so they can never disagree. It stays on the
  STATEMENT, never in the review panel: it is a bulk action over the current view, while the
  per-line Submit already lives in the panel footer (`exSubmit`), gated on coverage. Property and
  Cost center sit behind **More** (`FX_MOREDIMS`) but still count against Scope. Do not add
  controls the engine cannot act on (currency, owner, GAAP basis, movement type were specified
  and deliberately omitted). The review panel is a **full-height dock** in the Korvyn
  assistant's band: `sizeDrawer()` pins it below whatever occupies its column, so the
  full-width rows reserve that column instead — `.fx-work`/`.fx-focusline` at
  `--fx-dw-w + 12px`, `.topbar`/`.secnav`/`.subnav` at `+48px` (wider container). Declare
  these, never compute them from the drawer's own left edge — that is a feedback loop and the
  panel walks off-screen. `body.fx-mercury #fxRoot .fx-work` sets a later `margin` shorthand,
  so scope the reservation `#fxRoot.aw`; keep `.fxt` `white-space:nowrap`. **Full screen
  (`S.dwPage`) SWAPS per tab, it does not scroll** — `dwPageCols()` reads `RW_LAYOUT.drawer`
  so one registry drives both densities; Overview keeps the 3-column argument. All page chrome
  hides under `body.fx-dw-page`; the statement tabs need `!important` (paintSubnav sets
  `display` inline), and the class must be gated on `#fxRoot.offsetParent!==null` or it leaks
  to other pages. `--rule`/`--rule-strong` are redeclared in the dark block — ramp-derived
  aliases at `:root` otherwise substitute the LIGHT border and every dark hairline vanishes.
  `#tbScope` (the topbar entity tree) is **hidden on Flux** — it
  filters nothing here (`sliceOk()` never reads `F.entity`) and lists a different entity
  vocabulary than the Scope tab, so it could contradict the scope actually in force; hidden
  only on Flux, untouched elsewhere. `glHead()`/`periodNav()` no longer run on Flux: the header
  restated what the tabs state, and the month stepper became the Period field. ONE popover
  system (`#pop`) for every selector — anchored, never modal, one at a time, Escape/outside
  closes; multi-selects staged with Search · Included|Excluded · Select all · Clear/Apply,
  single-selects commit on click. Other pages keep the drawer —
  do not delete it. **Each tab's live-filter count is load-bearing**: tabs hide filters, and
  a signed statement must never be filtered for an invisible reason — do not drop the counts
  without replacing that guarantee. Each dimension menu is a
  **staged** multi-select (Search · Selected|Excluded · Select all · checkboxes · Apply):
  it edits `popDraft`/`popDraftX` and commits only in `popApply()`, so the consolidation
  is not recomputed per checkbox. Select all acts on the search-visible options only.
  `S.excl[dim]` is a REAL filter through `dimOk()` in `sliceOk()`, not a label — it rides
  in `snapshot()` beside the dimension arrays, and every scope renderer must spell the
  exclusion ("Excl. 2", "All except 2 entities"), because the same list under the flag
  means the opposite thing. An empty selection always clears the flag.
- **The explanation and the comments are ONE conversation** (2026-08-28, owner's direction).
  Every voice uses one grammar, `.rw-msg`: avatar in the gutter, identity on one line, words
  against a rail. The explanation is the lead statement (`.lead`); a Korvyn draft wears indigo
  `--ai`, never the cobalt accent; your own messages take `.mine`; awaited voices are hollow
  silhouettes (`.owed`); the composer is the last message (`.comp`), not the first. Order is
  load-bearing — thread, then composer, then who has not answered. `cmtField()` must never
  repaint (it toggles `.live` and autosizes in place, or the caret is lost). "Awaiting a
  comment" stays collapsed behind `S.cmtOwedOpen` with every action intact. **Replies are ONE
  level deep by design**: every message has a stable id (`k:<key>` for a lineage contribution,
  `id` for a discussion comment) and a reply stores `parent`; `cmtRootOf()` re-parents in
  `cmtSave()`, so the STORED shape can never exceed two levels. The reply box renders inside
  its group (`cmtComp` stands down while `S.cmtReplyTo` is set); the kind filter applies to
  ROOTS only, or replies orphan; the draft is discarded on aim/un-aim; and `S.cmtReplyTo` must
  clear in `pick()`, `pickAt()` and `closeDrawer()`. `cmtComp` is a
  registry part: add it to BOTH `RW_LAYOUT.drawer.comments` and `RW_LAYOUT.page.rec`. The base
  avatar rule is `#fxRoot.aw .fx-detail .rw-av`, so `.rw-av.ai` must match that specificity.
- A **materiality threshold** (`r.req`) decides whether a line must be explained at all. A
  **tolerance** (`r.clear`) decides whether an explanation covers enough of the movement.
  They are different numbers — conflating them is how a line below the threshold ends up
  permanently unsubmittable.
- Roles resolve at call time from `USER_ROLE` through `FX_CAPS`, keyed on
  `Accounting Manager` / `Controller` / `Chief Accounting Officer` / `Asset Manager` /
  `FP&A Analyst` / `External Auditor`. An unknown role falls back to view-only.
  Segregation of duties is enforced at the action (`canReviewLine`), not in the UI.

## Checks

```bash
node tools/check_chrome_themes.mjs
```

Then the browser sweep: open the file, drive every tab, assert each view rendered, console
clean.
